{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat ledger.jsonl'","aggregated_output":"{"seq": 1, "at": "2026-09-25T18:55:37Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "text": "Tentative bridge: Q classifies distributional predictions (P5) and costly predictor access (P4), while warning that no generic risk theorem transfers (Q lines 600-602). P gives a concrete risk functional: its empirical-CVaR gradient error is controlled by a CDF sup-norm bound (P Lemma cvar-estimation error bound, lines 1287-1293), feeding an ergodic optimization bound (P Theorem ergodic, lines 657ff). Candidate question: can a cheap predicted CDF at each agent be used with a small validation sample, while preserving P\\(\\)s bound under arbitrary prediction error? I am checking whether the apparent benefit survives validation cost and whether this is more than direct substitution.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 2, "at": "2026-09-25T18:55:48Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "intention", "text": "I am testing a resource-aware extension of P rather than treating P as a generic learning-augmented algorithm. Q \u00a7System-Level Implications and \u00a7Pricing the Prediction says prediction/sample cost is outside component objectives; P (eqs. sampling requirement, cvar gradient error sum, thm:ergodic) exposes finite-sample error and local query counts. Candidate: sharpen P\u2019s aggregate error to agent-specific terms, then optimize heterogeneous query allocation and, if justified, a learned CVaR oracle with a robust empirical fallback. I will check that this yields a genuinely different theorem and preserves P\u2019s filtration assumptions.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 3, "at": "2026-09-25T18:56:26Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "text": "Distinct from ada\\(\\)s sample-allocation line: use a scalar prediction as a *direction-independent centering value* in P\\(\\)s one-point estimator. At agent \\(i\\), before drawing \\(u_k^i\\), clip \\(b_k^i\\) to \\([-U_i,U_i]\\) and set \\(\\widetilde g_k^i=(d/\\delta_i)[\\widehat C_k^i(y_k^i+\\delta_i u_k^i)-b_k^i]u_k^i\\). Since \\(\\mathbb E[u_k^i\\mid\\mathcal F_k]=0\\), centering leaves the exact-CVaR smoothed-gradient identity intact for *any* prediction; clipping gives \\(\\|\\widetilde g_k^i\\|\\le 2dU_i/\\delta_i\\) even for adversarial predictions. If \\(\\epsilon_k^i=|b_k^i-C^i(y_k^i)|\\), then \\(\\mathbb E[\\|\\widetilde g_k^i\\|\\mid\\mathcal F_k]\\le dL_i+(d/\\delta_i)(\\epsilon_k^i+O(U_i/(\\alpha_i\\sqrt{s_k^i})))\\). Thus P\\(\\)s finite-horizon \\(1/\\delta_{\\min}^2\\) transient and graph disagreement could become error-sensitive, while P\\(\\)s finite-sample residual remains. Q lines 796 and 342 motivate hint-error accounting plus an arbitrary-advice cap; P lines 385-398 and 523-568 give the exact insertion point. Need check novelty against standard one-point baseline work and adapt P\\(\\)s pathwise disagreement proof to expected norms.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 4, "at": "2026-09-25T18:56:34Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Concrete robustifier for emmy\u2019s predicted-CDF idea: at P\u2019s perturbed point, take an \\(s\\)-sample empirical CDF \\(F_s\\), compare with a past-measurable valid predicted CDF \\(\\widetilde F\\), and use \\(\\widetilde F\\) iff \\(\\|\\widetilde F-F_s\\|_\\infty\\le 2b\\), otherwise use \\(F_s\\), with \\(b=\\sqrt{\\log(2mT/\\zeta)/(2s)}\\). Conditional DKW gives \\(\\|F_s-F\\|_\\infty\\le b\\) jointly through horizon \\(T\\) with probability at least \\(1-\\zeta\\). On that event, writing \\(\\eta=\\|\\widetilde F-F\\|_\\infty\\): if \\(\\eta\\le b\\), predictor is accepted and output CDF error is \\(\\eta\\); if \\(\\eta>b\\), an accepted predictor has error at most \\(3b\\), while rejected empirical error is at most \\(b\\). Thus output CDF error is at most \\(3\\min\\{\\eta,b\\}\\). P Appendix CVaR-CDF lemma converts this to CVaR error \\(\\le(6U_i/\\alpha_i)\\min\\{\\eta,b\\}\\), then its one-step proof transfers to an error-dependent ergodic bound. Caveat: validation still spends \\(s\\) samples, so no prediction-query saving follows; the novelty claim should be bound improvement and perhaps an explicit cost-quality frontier, not free sampling.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 5, "at": "2026-09-25T18:57:21Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "Cross-check of emmy\u2019s scalar-centering idea (#3): it is valid if \\(b_k^i\\) is \\(\\mathcal F_k\\)-measurable, hence independent of current \\(u_k^i\\) conditional on history; then \\(\\mathbb E[b_k^iu_k^i\\mid\\mathcal F_k]=0\\), so P\u2019s empirical-CVaR bias term is unchanged. With \\(\\epsilon_k^i=|b_k^i-C^i(y_k^i)|\\), P\u2019s \\(L_i\\)-Lipschitzness and Appendix DKW bound give \\(\\mathbb E[\\|\\widetilde g_k^i\\|^2\\mid\\mathcal F_k]\\le(2d^2/\\delta_i^2)[(\\epsilon_k^i+L_i\\delta_i)^2+4U_i^2/(\\alpha_i^2s_k^i)]\\). P\u2019s pathwise disagreement lemma cannot simply replace \\(G_i\\) by this expectation; its mixing recursion must be redone in expectation. Search found prior one-point residual feedback https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.07378 and current directional-hint control variates https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08277, so centering alone is unlikely novel; the possible contribution is the CVaR + dynamic network + arbitrary-hint error-sensitive theorem with an explicit query-cost comparison.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 6, "at": "2026-09-25T18:57:36Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "text": "A necessary comparison for #3: a prediction-free two-point/centered estimator can estimate \\(C^i(y_k^i)\\) with a second independent sample batch and subtract it. Its conditional mean is likewise unchanged because the baseline batch is independent of current direction, and it obtains the same \\(O(d^2[L_i^2+U_i^2/(\\alpha_i^2s\\delta_i^2)])\\) second-moment order for roughly twice the loss queries. Thus an exponent improvement over P\u2019s original one-point bound is not, by itself, a learning-augmented advantage; the case for prediction is saved baseline-query cost or stronger accuracy at a fixed total budget. P\u2019s oracle model must specify whether a second batch at the unperturbed point is allowed. Q \u00a7Pricing the Prediction requires charging the scalar-hint inference cost too.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 7, "at": "2026-09-25T18:59:04Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "I wrote ada/research-note.md with the forecast-centered estimator derivation, explicit moment bound, candidate \\(\\widetilde O(m\\varepsilon^{-6})\\) versus P-one-point \\(\\widetilde O(m\\varepsilon^{-10})\\) upper-bound comparison under accurate hints, and the decisive two-point baseline that matches the exponent with about twice the loss queries. The note also derives optimal heterogeneous allocation of P\u2019s existing CVaR samples: for \\(a_i=U_i/(\\alpha_i\\delta_i)\\) and per-query cost \\(\\kappa_i\\), minimizing \\(\\sum_i\\kappa_i s_i\\) subject to \\(\\sum_i a_i/\\sqrt{s_i}\\le B\\) gives \\(s_i=(a_i/\\kappa_i)^{2/3}(\\sum_j a_j^{2/3}\\kappa_j^{1/3}/B)^2\\). This allocation is a direct refinement but unlikely enough alone. The centered estimator seems the better paper question only if the oracle/cost model makes a cheap forecast genuinely preferable to a second sample batch.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 8, "at": "2026-09-25T18:59:23Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "text": "Do not transfer P\u2019s almost-sure last-iterate theorem to a time-varying forecast-centered method merely from the finite-horizon argument. P lines 857-978 require fixed \\(s^i\\) and a fixed expected empirical-smoothed surrogate; a history-dependent baseline has zero conditional mean, so it should not alter that surrogate if centered before \\(u_k^i\\), but the proof still needs fresh martingale/second-moment and disagreement checks. The safe initial claim is P-style finite-time expected ergodic suboptimality. Also Q\u2019s multiplicative consistency/robustness definitions (lines 298-343) do not directly apply to P\u2019s additive stochastic objective; call the result an error-dependent additive gap and arbitrary-hint cap.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
