{"seq": 10, "at": "2026-09-25T18:59:39Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "objection", "text": "Prior-work check weakens #3: Wang, Shen, Zavlanos, \"Risk-Averse No-Regret Learning in Online Convex Games\" (https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.08957) already analyzes one-point CVaR gradients using previous-iteration CVaR as residual feedback and proves a variance improvement in a game model. Q+P centering remains a valid derivation, but a paper needs to beat this and a fresh two-point baseline on total charged loss/predictor queries (ada #6), not just improve P\\(\\)s displayed \\(1/\\delta^2\\) term. Separate alternative I am checking: lift P\\(\\)s Rockafellar--Uryasev threshold \\(\\tau_i\\) into the optimization state, so one sampled loss gives an unbiased evaluation of the joint convex objective \\(\\tau_i+\\alpha_i^{-1}(J^i(x,\\xi)-\\tau_i)_+\\). This might remove P\\(\\)s empirical-CVaR bias without growing per-round batches; a predicted quantile could warm-start \\(\\tau_i\\). Need check prior work and distributed proof before claiming an advance.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 11, "at": "2026-09-25T19:00:25Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "intention", "text": "Reopened by emmy #10. I am testing the lifted Rockafellar--Uryasev objective \\(\\phi_i(x,\\tau_i)=\\tau_i+\\alpha_i^{-1}\\mathbb E[(J^i(x,\\xi^i)-\\tau_i)_+]\\) against P\\(\\)s exact noisy point-query oracle. I will check a one-sample unbiased joint stochastic update, the price of its \\(1/\\delta_i^2\\) variance, threshold projection and network coupling, then compare with prior CVaR zeroth-order methods. Scalar centering remains a useful control variate but prior residual-feedback work weakens novelty.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 12, "at": "2026-09-25T19:00:28Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "text": "Sharper alternative to P\\(\\)s plug-in CVaR estimator: keep each local Rockafellar--Uryasev threshold \\(\\tau_i\\in[-U_i,U_i]\\) as a decision variable and optimize \\(\\Phi(x,\\tau)=m^{-1}\\sum_i\\mathbb E[\\tau_i+\\alpha_i^{-1}(J^i(x,\\xi)-\\tau_i)_+]\\). P states this representation in \u00a7CVaR (P lines 310-329), but minimizes over \\(\\tau\\) afresh inside every sampled CVaR value (P lines 404-440), creating its \\(e_s/\\delta\\) bias. Under P\\(\\)s convexity assumption, the lifted sample loss is convex in \\((x,\\tau_i)\\). One fresh \\(J^i(x+\\delta u,\\xi)\\) gives a one-point x-gradient estimate of its smoothed lifted objective and a \u03c4-subgradient \\(1-\\alpha_i^{-1}\\mathbf 1\\{J^i>\\tau_i\\}\\); these are conditionally unbiased, so no plug-in CVaR error term. Predicted quantiles can initialize or hint \u03c4, with projection retaining arbitrary-prediction guarantees. Preliminary central rate from bounded one-point estimates is \\(O(1/(\\delta\\sqrt T)+\\delta/\\alpha_{\\min})\\), optimized as \\(O(T^{-1/4})\\) using only \\(T\\) loss samples per agent. Need prove distributed x-consensus/local-\u03c4 recursion and check prior art; Q\\(\\)s query-cost lens makes the one-sample comparison meaningful.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 13, "at": "2026-09-25T19:01:16Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "text": "My #7 forecast-centering query exponent comparison used the fixed schedule \\(\\eta_k\\asymp k^{-1/2}\\), so it is not an optimized complexity comparison. With \\(\\delta\\asymp\\varepsilon\\), \\(s\\asymp\\varepsilon^{-4}\\), and horizon-aware constant \\(\\eta\\asymp\\delta/\\sqrt T\\), P\\(\\)s terms \\(D^2/(T\\eta)+\\eta G^2\\) for \\(G=O(1/\\delta)\\) balance at \\(O(1/(\\delta\\sqrt T))\\). Its displayed bound then suggests \\(T=\\widetilde O(\\varepsilon^{-4})\\) and \\(\\widetilde O(m\\varepsilon^{-8})\\) loss queries, not my earlier \\(\\varepsilon^{-10}\\). The graph term also needs this schedule checked but is smaller under geometric mixing. This is an upper-bound comparison, not a lower bound.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 14, "at": "2026-09-25T19:01:36Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "objection", "text": "Subtle issue in lifted-\\(\\tau\\) claim #12: the one-point \\(x\\)-estimator at \\(y+\\delta u\\), \\(u\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}(\\mathbb S^{d-1})\\), is unbiased for the \\(x\\)-gradient of *ball*-smoothed \\(\\Phi_i\\), but the simultaneous indicator \\(1-\\alpha_i^{-1}\\mathbf1\\{J(y+\\delta u,\\xi)>\\tau\\}\\) is a \\(\\tau\\)-subgradient of the *sphere*-smoothed \\(\\Phi_i\\). They are not generally gradients of the same smoothed joint objective. Repair: because \\(J^i(\\cdot,\\xi)\\) is \\(L_i\\)-Lipschitz, both smoothings differ from the unsmoothed \\(\\Phi_i\\) by at most \\(L_i\\delta_i/\\alpha_i\\); use separate convexity inequalities and pay an additional \\(O(L_i\\delta_i/\\alpha_i)\\) approximate-subgradient term. Alternatively a second unperturbed loss query gives an exact \\(\\tau\\)-subgradient of the original joint objective.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 15, "at": "2026-09-25T19:02:33Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "idea", "text": "Exact one-sample repair for #14: draw \\(v\\) from the compact-support density \\(p(v)\\propto(1-\\|v\\|^2)^2\\mathbf1_{\\{\\|v\\|<1\\}}\\). Let \\(H_i(x,\\tau,\\xi)=\\tau+\\alpha_i^{-1}(J^i(x,\\xi)-\\tau)_+\\), and smooth \\(\\bar\\Phi_i(y,\\tau)=\\mathbb E_{v,\\xi}H_i(y+\\delta_i v,\\tau,\\xi)\\). One loss sample at \\(y+\\delta_i v\\) gives \\(g_x=4\\alpha_i^{-1}(J^i-\\tau)_+v/[\\delta_i(1-\\|v\\|^2)]\\) by the score identity, and \\(g_\\tau=1-\\alpha_i^{-1}\\mathbf1\\{J^i>\\tau\\}\\). Conditional means form a joint subgradient of the SAME convex smoothed objective; \\(\\mathbb E\\|4v/(1-\\|v\\|^2)\\|^2=2d(d+4)\\), so \\(\\mathbb E\\|g_x\\|^2\\le 8d(d+4)U_i^2/(\\alpha_i^2\\delta_i^2)\\), while \\(|g_\\tau|\\le 1/\\alpha_i\\). Smoothing bias is \\(\\le L_i\\delta_i/\\alpha_i\\). This avoids the sphere/ball mismatch without a second oracle query, but network disagreement now needs an expected-moment proof because the score is unbounded near the ball boundary.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 16, "at": "2026-09-25T19:03:27Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "finding", "text": "For the compact-kernel lifted method (#15), P\\(\\)s graph and projection proof can be rebuilt in expectation: with \\(e_k^i=x_{k+1}^i-y_k^i\\), nonexpansive projection gives \\(\\mathbb E\\|e_k^i\\|\\le\\eta_k\\sqrt{\\mathbb E\\|g_{x,k}^i\\|^2}\\); geometric mixing then gives mean disagreement \\(O(\\eta/\\delta)\\) for constant \\(\\eta\\). A product-state Lyapunov \\(\\sum_i(\\|y_k^i-z\\|^2+|\\tau_k^i-\\theta_i|^2)\\), with x only mixed and local thresholds projected to \\([-U_i,U_i]\\), yields a finite-horizon expected gap of order \\(O(A/(\\eta T)+\\eta B/\\delta^2+\\eta/\\delta+\\delta)\\), where \\(A\\) includes initial threshold distances and \\(B\\) includes \\(d,\\alpha_i,U_i\\). Choosing \\(\\eta\\asymp\\delta/\\sqrt T\\), \\(\\delta\\asymp T^{-1/4}\\) gives \\(O(T^{-1/4})\\) with one loss query per agent per iteration and no \\(e_s/\\delta\\) residual. This is an achievable upper-bound route, not a proved novelty or lower bound. Q \u00a7Pricing the Prediction motivates comparing charged queries; P \u00a7CVaR, eq:EDF, and thm:ergodic give the exact baseline. Related primary literature to check: Cardoso--Xu stochastic CVaR bandits https://proceedings.mlr.press/v89/cardoso19a/cardoso19a.pdf and Wang et al. zeroth-order CVaR games https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.02838. Searches included \"CVaR zeroth-order Rockafellar one sample\" and \"CVaR bandit auxiliary convex threshold\"; no absence-of-prior-work claim follows.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 17, "at": "2026-09-25T19:03:34Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "text": "Ada #14 is right: my #12/notes incorrectly combined an x-gradient of ball-smoothed \\(H_i\\) with a \u03c4-subgradient at a sphere-perturbed point. Simpler one-query repair than a score kernel: smooth the *joint* variable \\(z_i=(x,\\tau_i)\\in\\mathbb R^{d+1}\\) with a ball and draw \\(u_i\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}(\\mathbb S^d)\\). Query one \\(J^i(y_i+\\delta u_{x,i},\\xi)\\), evaluate \\(H_i(y_i+\\delta u_{x,i},\\tau_i+\\delta u_{\\tau,i},\\xi)\\), and set \\(g_i=(d+1)H_i u_i/\\delta\\). The sphere identity now makes the *whole* vector conditionally unbiased for the gradient of the same jointly smoothed convex objective. Perturbed \u03c4 may leave \\([-U_i,U_i]\\), but \\(H_i\\) is defined there and bounded on \\([-U_i-\\delta,U_i+\\delta]\\); project stored \u03c4 back into \\([-U_i,U_i]\\). This retains a pathwise \\(O((d+1)U_i/(\\alpha_i\\delta))\\) norm cap, so P-style graph mixing remains applicable. It may have worse \u03c4 variance than ada #15\\(\\)s score scheme but is a clean first proof route.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 18, "at": "2026-09-25T19:05:06Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "text": "Emmy #17 is the cleaner primary route. I cross-checked it: joint smoothing in \\(z=(x,\\tau)\\in\\mathbb R^{d+1}\\) with \\(u\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}(\\mathbb S^d)\\) yields \\(\\mathbb E[(d+1)H_i((y,\\tau)+\\delta u,\\xi)u/\\delta\\mid\\mathcal F_k]=\\nabla\\bar\\Phi_i(y,\\tau)\\) for one shared \\((d+1)\\)-ball-smoothed convex function; only the x component requires a loss query, and \\(\\tau+\\delta u_\\tau\\) need not be an oracle input. Stored \\(\\tau\\in[-U_i,U_i]\\), so sample \\(H_i\\) is bounded for \\(\\delta\\le U_i\\), giving a pathwise gradient cap and P-style graph mixing. My #14 suggestion that separate ball/sphere partial convexity inequalities alone suffice was not justified: separate partial subgradients need not combine into a joint subgradient. #15 remains a valid but more technical one-query alternative. I now prefer #17 for the lead theorem.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 19, "at": "2026-09-25T19:05:08Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Detailed corrected derivation is in emmy/lifted-threshold.md. With joint spherical perturbation \\(u_i\\in\\mathbb S^d\\) of \\((x,\\tau_i)\\), one queried loss yields an unbiased gradient of the same joint ball-smoothed convex \\(H_i\\), with pathwise norm \\(\\le(d+1)M_{i,\\delta}/\\delta\\), \\(M_{i,\\delta}=U_i+\\delta+(2U_i+\\delta)/\\alpha_i\\). For a centralized projected update, \\(\\mathbb E[\\mathcal C(\\bar x_T)]-\\mathcal C^\\star\\le D_zG_\\delta/\\sqrt T+2L_z\\delta+L_H D_x\\delta/r\\), where \\(G_\\delta=O(1/\\delta)\\), \\(L_z=m^{-1}\\sum_i\\sqrt{L_i^2+1}/\\alpha_i\\), and \\(L_H=m^{-1}\\sum_i L_i/\\alpha_i\\). Thus \\(\\delta\\asymp T^{-1/4}\\) gives \\(O(T^{-1/4})\\) with one loss query per agent per iteration and no \\(e_s/\\delta\\) plateau. Distributed graph extension has an explicit potential/mixing sketch in the note; it is not yet a full proof. Crucial limitation: this one-query improvement uses P\\(\\)s CVaR representation, while Q mainly supplies prediction/cost framing. A forecast only improves initial-threshold constants unless it has a stronger oracle contract, so a pair-specific learning-augmented publication claim remains unresolved.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 20, "at": "2026-09-25T19:06:02Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "A clean limitation on pair-specific prediction benefit (proof in emmy/lifted-threshold.md): in P\\(\\)s one-agent subclass with \\(\\mathcal X=[-1,1]\\), \\(\\xi\\sim\\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\\alpha)\\), let world A have \\(J_A(x,\\xi)=\\xi(x+1)/2\\), world B have \\(J_B(x,\\xi)=\\xi(1-x)/2\\). All P boundedness/convexity assumptions hold. Both worlds have the same *exact* upper-tail VaR threshold \\(\\tau^*(x)=0\\) for every \\(x\\), but \\(C_A(x)=(x+1)/2\\) and \\(C_B(x)=(1-x)/2\\) have opposite minimizers. On the event of no tail observation in \\(n\\) adaptive queries, probability \\((1-\\alpha)^n\\), histories are identical; averaged over worlds any output has gap \\(1/2\\). Hence worst-world expected gap is at least \\((1-\\alpha)^n/2\\) even with perfect threshold advice. This does not settle all prediction models, but it proves that a quantile-only hint cannot by itself save the rare-tail samples needed for a small CVaR gap. A candidate publication must specify richer tail-magnitude advice or different oracle access, and compare against the one-query lifted baseline.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 21, "at": "2026-09-25T19:06:06Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "correction", "text": "Decisive prior-art correction: Cardoso and Xu (2019), *Risk-Averse Stochastic Convex Bandit*, Algorithm 1 and Theorem 2, https://proceedings.mlr.press/v89/cardoso19a/cardoso19a.pdf pp. 3-4, already give EXACTLY the joint \\((x,\\tau)\\) sphere perturbation in dimension \\(d+1\\), one observed loss, estimator \\((d+1)H(x+\\delta u_x,\\tau+\\delta u_\\tau)u/\\delta\\), and \\(O(T^{-1/4})\\) expected pseudo-regret. Thus emmy #17/#18 is a valid bridge into P\\(\\)s dynamic network, but the estimator, lifted threshold, one-query insight, and exponent are NOT new. A publishable claim would need a nontrivial distributed result, tight communication/query tradeoff, or prediction-sensitive improvement beyond simply inserting their algorithm into P\\(\\)s consensus template. I am checking that narrower possibility; the literature search did find direct prior work, not a gap.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 22, "at": "2026-09-25T19:06:51Z", "actor": "ada", "kind": "idea", "text": "Strengthening emmy #20: exact quantile advice has a quantitative tail-sample lower bound. One agent, \\(x\\in[-1,1]\\), draw \\(E\\sim\\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\\alpha)\\) and independent \\(S\\in\\{-1,+1\\}\\) with \\(\\Pr_\\pm(S=1)=1/2\\pm\\gamma\\); return \\(J(x,E,S)=E(1+Sx)/2\\). Both worlds satisfy P\\(\\)s bounded convex loss assumptions and have exact lower \\((1-\\alpha)\\)-quantile \\(0\\) at every \\(x\\), but \\(C_\\pm(x)=1/2\\pm\\gamma x\\) have opposite minimizers. By data processing, each adaptive noisy loss query has KL at most \\(\\alpha D(\\mathrm{Bern}(1/2+\\gamma)\\|\\mathrm{Bern}(1/2-\\gamma))\\le16\\alpha\\gamma^2\\) for \\(\\gamma\\le1/4\\). Thus \\(n\\) transcript KL \\(\\le16n\\alpha\\gamma^2\\), and Pinsker gives TV \\(\\le1/2\\) for \\(n\\le1/(32\\alpha\\gamma^2)\\). The average of the two expected CVaR gaps is at least \\(\\gamma(1-\\mathrm{TV})\\ge\\gamma/2\\). Taking \\(\\gamma=2\\varepsilon\\) proves \\(\\Omega(1/(\\alpha\\varepsilon^2))\\) necessary noisy loss calls for worst-world expected gap below \\(\\varepsilon\\), despite perfect quantile advice. This is only a threshold-hint lower bound, not a full zeroth-order minimax rate; it cannot alone establish publication novelty.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 23, "at": "2026-09-25T19:06:57Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "correction", "text": "Prior-art correction to #19 and emmy/lifted-threshold.md: Cardoso and Xu, \"Risk-Averse Stochastic Convex Bandit\" (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v89/cardoso19a/cardoso19a.pdf), Algorithm 1 on pp. 3-4 and Theorem 2 on p. 4, already use the identical joint \\((x,\\tau)\\) spherical estimator, one loss query, and \\(O(T^{-1/4})\\) centralized expected pseudo-regret. I verified the primary PDF. Our derivation establishes no new estimator or rate. The only possible extension is P\\(\\)s changing network, perhaps with a richer Q-style prediction contract; even that needs a prior-art check, complete proof, and query/communication comparison. The exact-quantile lower-bound witness #20 limits claims that VaR advice alone reduces tail-query cost.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 24, "at": "2026-09-25T19:07:25Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Cross-check of ada #22: valid under P\\(\\)s oracle. For \\(J=E(1+Sx)/2\\), conditional on a tail draw \\(E=1\\), the upper-tail CVaR is \\((1+\\mathbb E S\\,x)/2\\), including endpoint atoms; hence the two worlds have opposite linear objectives but identical exact VaR \\(0\\). An adaptive transcript is a data-processed version of the i.i.d. \\((E,S)\\) samples, so KL per loss observation is at most \\(\\alpha D(\\mathrm{Bern}(1/2+\\gamma)\\|\\mathrm{Bern}(1/2-\\gamma))\\le16\\alpha\\gamma^2\\). Pinsker gives the stated \\(\\Omega(1/(\\alpha\\varepsilon^2))\\) query lower bound for fixed target gap. This improves my cruder no-tail witness #20; both show that exact threshold prediction alone has weak informational content for CVaR optimization. The publication-level question is now sharper: what tail-magnitude prediction object and charged query model can beat a Cardoso--Xu one-query baseline on P\\(\\)s network while retaining an arbitrary-advice cap? We have no positive theorem yet.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
