{"seq": 28, "at": "2026-09-25T19:11:04Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "idea", "text": "Additional avenue after #26: allow a diminishing joint smoothing radius in the known Cardoso--Xu one-query estimator, while P-style mixing acts only on the shared (x) and each agent keeps its private threshold. A proof route for almost-sure exact last-iterate consensus is in emmy/vanishing-radius.md. For (delta_kasymp(k+1)^{-p}), (eta_kasymp(k+1)^{-q}), conditions (q+p>1) and (q-p>1/2), with (qle1), make smoothing error (sum_keta_kdelta_k), gradient noise (sum_keta_k^2/delta_k^2), and graph error (sum_keta_kD_k) summable while (sum_keta_k=infty); (p=1/4,q=5/6) works. A moving comparator in P\\(\\)s shrinking feasible set has finite total motion. This suggests exact optimality without P\\(\\)s fixed-radius, fixed-batch residual (P lines 854-978). However, the full Robbins--Siegmund and random-cluster-point proof is not complete, and novelty is uncertain: Cardoso--Xu already supply the estimator (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v89/cardoso19a/cardoso19a.pdf), and Li--Assaad already analyze one-point distributed zeroth-order convex optimization on time-varying networks (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.12597). Q \u00a7Pricing the Prediction (lines 1235-1258) motivates charging queries, but no forecast improves this route. Searches: distributed CVaR bandit zeroth order one point almost sure convergence time varying network auxiliary threshold; decentralized risk averse CVaR bandit stochastic convex optimization one sample last iterate convergence; distributed stochastic convex optimization diminishing smoothing radius one point bandit almost sure convergence time varying graphs. No novelty claim follows.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 29, "at": "2026-09-25T19:11:28Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Small strengthening of the threshold-advice lower bound in #22/#24: the two worlds also have the same exact FULL loss distribution at the natural anchor (x=0), namely mass (1-alpha) at (0) and mass (alpha) at (1/2). Hence even giving the exact CDF and CVaR value at (x=0), together with the exact VaR function at every (x), leaves the (Omega(1/(alpha\u000barepsilon^2))) query lower bound unchanged. The reason is informational: the sign parameter enters only the tail response to moving (x). This is a sharper boundary for Q P5 distributional advice than quantile-only advice, though it still does not address a predictor supplying CDFs uniformly over all decisions. The witness and KL proof are in ada/lifted-threshold.md, based on P \u00a7CVaR (lines 327-339) and P loss-oracle contract (lines 404-423).", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 30, "at": "2026-09-25T19:12:03Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Current best account of Q+P: sharpen the assigned broad question to what tail information a charged prediction must reveal to improve P\\(\\)s distributed CVaR loss-query complexity beyond a one-query lifted baseline. Q distinguishes distributional predictions and warns that they do not imply a generic coherent-risk theorem (\u00a7P5, lines 595-602), and it calls for charging predictor calls (\u00a7Pricing the Prediction, lines 1235-1258). P gives a concrete CVaR threshold representation (lines 327-339), independent noisy loss oracle (lines 404-423), and a batch-estimation residual (lines 657-795). Under that oracle, the two-world witness in #22/#24/#29 proves that exact VaR advice for every decision, even plus an exact CDF at (x=0), still requires (Omega(1/(alpha\u000barepsilon^2))) loss queries for expected gap below (\u000barepsilon); it leaves tail magnitudes unknown. Positive one-query threshold lifting has already been done centrally by Cardoso--Xu (https://proceedings.mlr.press/v89/cardoso19a/cardoso19a.pdf), while Li--Assaad (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2105.12597) handle general one-point distributed optimization on changing networks. Thus no pair-specific publishable positive result is yet established. The open research target is a precise, charged tail-response prediction contract with a matching error-dependent upper/lower comparison against that baseline; alternatively, finish and prior-check the exact last-iterate distributed theorem sketched in emmy/vanishing-radius.md. The witness lower bound does not match the known (O(\u000barepsilon^{-4})) upper route, and the last-iterate proof and novelty remain unresolved.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
